Abstract
Nanocellulose, as the star nanomaterial in carbohydrate polymers, has excellent mechanical properties, biodegradability, and easy chemical modification. However, further practical applications of nanocellulose are limited by their inadequate functionalization. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as the star nanomaterial in functional polymers, have a large surface area, high porosity, and adjustable structure. The collaboration of nanocellulose and MOFs is a desirable strategy to make composites especially interesting for multifunctional and multi-field applications. What sparks will be produced by the collaboration of two-star nanomaterials? In this review article, we highlight an up-to-date overview of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites. The sewage treatment, gas separation, energy storage, and biomedical applications are mainly summarized. Finally, the challenges and research trends of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites are prospected. We hope this review may provide a valuable reference for the development and applications of carbohydrate polymer composites soon.
Keywords: Nanocellulose; Metal-organic framework; Composites; Applications;
## 1 Introduction
As the concept of sustainable development continues to gain popularity currently, there is a growing interest in renewable carbohydrate polymer materials to gradually replace petrochemical hydrocarbon compounds (Huang et al., 2022a; Mohanty, Vivekanandhan, Pin, & Misra, 2018; Zhai et al., 2022). Nanocellulose, as a critical renewable material, has the characteristics of biodegradability, excellent mechanical properties, abundant resources, and easy chemical modification (Du et al., 2021a; Oksman et al., 2016). However, due to the relatively simple functional groups on the surface of nanocellulose, it is challenging to meet the strict requirements of industrial development (Liu et al., 2021a). To improve the performance and application range of nanocellulose, compounding with functional materials is a profitable strategy (Xin et al., 2019; Yang et al., 2021). Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have received much attention because of the characteristics of large specific surface area, high porosity, tunable pore size, and ordered structure (Wang et al., 2022a). In addition, the rich choice of metals or metal oxides and ligands makes MOFs especially interesting for gas storage, separation, chemical sensing, and biomedical applications (Chu et al., 2022; Xiong, Li, You, Cao, & Cao, 2020). However, the vast majority kinds of MOFs are in the form of crystalline powders. Besides, MOFs also have defects, long-range disorders, and low conductivity (Dědek et al., 2022; Morris & Brammer, 2017; Ren et al., 2017), which limited the further development.
The shaping technologies were applied to improve the structure and property of monolithic MOFs composites (Lorignon, Gossard, & Carboni, 2020; Qi, Liu, & Chang, 2022). In the previous reports, most of the MOFs were mainly concentrated in traditional non-biodegradable polymers (Amini, Ebrahimzadeh, Seidi, & Jalilian, 2021; Chen, Vinh-Thang, Rodrigue, &
Kaliaguine, 2012; Lee, Seo, & Jang, 2017; Vinodh et al., 2022). Therefore, the development of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites is of great significance for broadening and improving their industrial applications (Tu, Ding, & Keplinger, 2022). There are some review articles about carbohydrate polymer-based MOFs composite, such as chitosan (Musarurwa & Tavengwa, 2022), enzyme (Lian et al., 2017), wood (Ma et al., 2022a), and cellulose (Abdelhamid & Mathew, 2022; Kim, Otal, & Hinestroza, 2019; Liu et al., 2021b; Zhang, Wang, Ding, Feng, & Yao, 2021). However, there are few reports on the in-depth discussion of nanocellulose, in the review article by Lu et al. (2022), they focused on the preparation approaches, general properties, and emerging applications of MOFs and cellulose nanomaterial composites. In addition, MOFs as functional substances were highlighted in most review papers, which provides meaningful insights for the development of nanocellulose based MOFs composites. In recent years, the number of published papers on nanocellulose-based MOFs composites have been increased rapidly (Fig. 1a). In order to highlight the role of nanocellulose, based on the latest progress of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites, this review paper will help to deepen the understanding of the mechanisms, expand its applications fields, and highlight the role of nanocellulose.
In this review article, the latest developments of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites are summarized, focusing on the roles of synthetic methods, interaction, structural characteristics, and properties (Fig. 1b). The advanced applications for environment, energy, and biomedical applications of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites are mainly described (Fig. 2). In particular, we pay attention to the functionalization and mechanism between MOFs and nanocellulose. Additionally, we highlight the role of nanocellulose in nanocellulose-based MOFs composites. At last, the current challenges and prospects of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites are proposed. This review is expected to provide a valuable reference for the development and applications of carbohydrate polymer composites in the near future.
