## 2 Development of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites
Nanocellulose is a macromolecular carbohydrate polymer composed of the D-glucose with $\upbeta$ -1,4-glucoside bond (Li, Wang, Ma, & Wang, 2018; Zeng et al., 2021a). Generally speaking, nanocellulose can be divided into three major categories based on the preparation methods: CNFs and CNCs were obtained via the top-down strategy involving specific chemical or physical treatment of cellulose. Meanwhile, BCs were cultivated via the bottom-up approach from glucose by bacteria under particular conditions (Chen et al., 2021a). As displayed in Fig. 3a, b, and Table 1, three types of nanocellulo $\mathbf{\hat{\mu}}^{\mathbf{\sigma}}\mathbf{\hat{e}}$ have different sources and sizes. CNFs are composed of a large number of long chain structure nanocellulose networks, which can obtain from plants via mechanical treatment or chemical pretreatments (Liu et al., 2022a). 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl radical (TEMPO)-oxidized CNFs are more popular than those treated by conventional mechanical processes due to the characteristics of the energy-saving process, better dispersion, and larger aspect ratio (Wang et al., 2022b). CNCs usually possess the rod-like or needle-like shape with dimension of less $70\mathrm{nm}$ in width and few $250~\mathrm{nm}$ in length, depending on the source of cellulose. In particular, tunicates are the only animal source of cellulose, so CNCs extracted from tunicates is special and the width are much longer than $250~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter (Wu et al., 2022a). BCs have a curved rope-like appearance between 20 and $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in width and the length is similar to the CNFs. BCs have high cellulose content in purity, which do not contain lignin or hemicellulose (Guo et al., 2022). At present, nanocellulose has been applied in a wide range of fields, such as textile (Ma et al., 2021a), energy storage (Xu et al., 2021), food packaging (Deng et al., 2022a), and water purification (Yuan, Huang, Wang, Mai, & Ma, 2022).
Table 1. The typical sources and average sizes of nanocellulose (Klemm et al., 2011; Sharma, Sharma, Lindstrom, & Hsiao, 2020; Yuan, Bian, & Ma, 2021)
MOFs are a kind of porous material after inorganic zeolite molecular sieve and organic activated carbon. The concept of MOFs was first proposed by Yaghi’s group in 1995 and developed rapidly in the following decades (Yaghi & Li, 1995). In general, MOFs are constructed by two parts: inorganic metal or metallic oxide centers and organic ligands, which have both rigidity of inorganic component and the flexibility of organic component (Fig. 3c). These two parts are bonded by strong coordination bonds to form the 1D, 2D or 3D unique periodic pore reticular formation (Fig. 3d) (Annamalai et al., 2022; Kang, Fan, & Sun, 2017; Wu, Wan, Li, Zhang, & Zhang, 2022). The MOFs are classified according to the structural characteristics (e.g., ZIFs, IRMOFs, and MTV-MOFs) or research institutions (e.g., MIL, HKUST, and UiO). Up to now, thousands of MOFs have been reported (Helal, Yamani, Cordova, & Yaghi, 2017; Mai & Liu, 2019; Stock & Biswas, 2012; Zhang et al., 2021a). Different types of MOFs have their unique synthesis methods. In the case of the classic ZIF-8 material, Lee et al. (2015) compared the properties of particles produced by different synthesis methods (solvothermal, microwave-assisted, sonochemical, mechanochemical, dry-gel, and microfluidic methods) of ZIF-8. They found that there was almost similar in structural properties. However, the energy consumption, complexity and yield of the preparation process are different (Sud & Kaur, 2021). MOFs were constructed from organic ligands of different lengths and central metal nodes of various forms of coordination, encompassing void space to endow large surface area (Furukawa, Cordova, O'Keeffe, & Yaghi, 2013; Jian et al., 2021), such as MOF-5 $(2320\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Li, Eddaoudi, O'Keeffe, & Yaghi, 1999) and NU-110 (From $7000\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $10500\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ (Farha et al., 2012). The fascinating features of MOFs resulted in widely applications in the fields of sewage treatment (Li et al., 2018), gas separation (Li, Sculley, & Zhou, 2012), catalysis (Fan et al., 2021), luminescent materials (Cui, Yue, Qian, & Chen, 2012), and sensor (Yang et al., 2022b).
There are two methods to prepare nanocellulose-based MOFs composites, ex-situ blending and in-situ growth. Nanocellulose acts as a good matrix for filling MOFs powder or a suitable skeleton for growing MOFs crystals. Ex-situ blending (through mixing fills and the polymer matrix in the solvent or solution) mainly refers to the preparation of MOFs materials as fills in advance, and then compositing with nanocellulose (Gong, Zhou, Qian, Shi, & Yu, 2021; Li, Yu, Sun, & Lu, 2015). In situ growth is to cross-link nanocellulose with metal ions, and then add ligands to make MOFs grow on the surface of nanocellulose (Lu et al., 2022). Abdelhamid, Georgouvelas, Edlund, & Mathew (2022) compared the effects of in-situ and ex-situ of ZIFs on the properties of cellulose-ZIF mixed $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathbf{r}}$ aper, and proved that the in-situ procedure is consistently better than the ones prepared via ex-situ procedure. Ma et al. (2019a) used BC aerogels as template, which were exposed to the metal ions solution, and then added organic ligand solution. The rich hydroxyl groups in BC can adsorb metal ions through weak interaction, and then make MOF nanoparticles nucleate on the surface of nanocellulose. In contrast, if metal ion solution is mixed with organic ligand solution and used to prepare BC composite aerogel, MOF particles will grow on BC surface in a non-uniform way due to the weakening of electrostatic attraction between BC and metal ions.
The composites can be further processed into films, hydrogels, and aerogels. Vacuum filtration is a common method for preparing membrane composites. The prepared membrane material has the characteristics of lightness, flexibility, and good process ability. In addition, the particularity of membrane material plays a vital role in the field of battery and gas filtration (Das, Choi, Nguyen, Kim, & Yoon, 2022; Lau & Yong, 2021). Due to the surface charge and the presence of hydrophilic functional groups, nanocellulose will self-aggregate and form a gel-like substance (Hu, Zhi, Shan, & Ni, 2022). The hydrogel is a stretchable porous network gel, in which the gaps are filled with liquid, and aerogel is a lightweight porous network gel, in which the gaps are filled with gas (Huang et al., 2022b; Zhang et al., 2021b). Hydrogel can be converted to aerogel by freeze-drying or supercritical carbon dioxide drying using $_2\cdot\mathrm{as}$ instead of liquid components in the hydrogel (Chen et al., 2019b; He, Zhang, Meng, & Wu, 2017). And aerogels can rehydrate to form hydrogels with new pore morphology and physical properties (De France, Hoare, & Cranston, 2017). Chen et al. (2021b) proved that the porosity and structure of nanocellulose can be controlled by adjusting the parameters of freeze-drying and regulating the growth of ice crystals. The research solves the problem of uneven pore structure easily formed by the traditional directional freezing method, and realizes the transformation from local orientation to long-distance orientation, which is of great significance to the development of nanocellulose aerogel materials. Nanocellulose and MOFs are compounded together in advance, and then the different components are bonded together under the condition of vacuum pressure, thus forming a membrane (Zhu et al., 2022). Whether preparing membranes, hydrogels or aerogels, MOFs can be pre-mixed with nanocellulose by ex-situ blending or the nanocellulose framework can be prepared and in situ growth MOFs. Therefore, different methods can be selected according to the experimental conditions and the characteristics of the materials.
MOFs can be combined with other materials by electrostatic interaction or hydrogen bonding (Chen et al., 2022a; Li et al., 2021; Ma & Urban, 2019). Although the composite of MOFs materials and graphene, MXene, carbon nanotubes, and other materials can also improve the performance, the shape problem is still not solved, and nanocellulose materials can be used as a framework to give MOF materials more machinability. Furthermore, 1D nanocellulose can be used as a templet for preparing the nanocellulose-based MOFs composites by many methods. Besides, nanocellulose materials have better degradability than traditional polymers. Compared with other template materials, nanocellulose has higher aspect ratio, surface carboxyl and hydroxyl groups, and modification, which is helpful to provide binding sites for nucleation and growth of MOFs (Li et al., 2022). What kind of sparks will be produced from the collaboration of two worlds of star materials? What is the interaction mechanism between nanocellulose and porous coordination polymers MOFs? In the following sections, we will provide an up-to-date overview of property, mechanism, and applications of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites.
## 3 Nanocellulose-based MOFs composites for sewage disposal
Wastewater usually contains complex components, such as microorganisms, heavy metal ions, and complex organic matter, which makes it difficult to be purified (Liu et al., 2016). Adsorption and degradation are two common solutions to solve the water pollution (Chenab, Sohrabi, Jafari, & Ramakrishna, 2020). Generally, physical adsorption and chemical adsorption are two kinds of main adsorption processes (Yagub, Sen, Afroze, & Ang, 2014). The adsorption capacity of the adsorbent depends largely on the specific surface area, so the porous MOFs have application potential as adsorption materials (Rouquerol, Rouquerol, & Sing, 1999; Shi, Wang, Lang, Zhou, & Ma, 2022). Degradation is another effective way to remove contaminants from water, which is to make toxic water pollutants harmless by oxidizing them with highly reactive free radicals or substances with strong oxidizing capacity (Feng et al., 2021). Hence, the MOFs can be used as degradation materials due to the abundant active site centers. The nanocellulose-based MOFs composites were developed to solve the growing water pollution problem due to the high specific surface area, diversified structure, and adjustable pore size (Gu et al., 2021; Nasrollahzadeh, Sajjadi, Iravani, & Varma, 2021; Yusuf et al., 2020). Rapid progress had paid to the synthesis of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites for sewage disposal, including heavy metal ions contamination, dyes, and pharmaceutical contamination, and oil contaminants (Zhao, Chen, & Huang, 2019).
## 3.1 Heavy metal ions contamination
At a certain concentration, all heavy metal ions are almost toxic (Naushad et al., 2019). Heavy metal ions in the human body can strongly interact with proteins and inactivate various enzymes (Bansod, Kumar, Thakur, Rana, & Singh, 2017). Owing to metal nodes and ligands based on MOFs and surface group properties of nanocellulose, nanocellulose-based MOFs composites provide a solution to solve the problem of heavy metal pollution. For example, Kobielska and co-workers (2018) reported the adsorption mechanism of heavy metal ions by different kinds of MOFs via ion exchange, electrostatic interaction, and coordination. Besides, Voisin et al. (2017) revealed the adsorption mechanism of heavy metal ions by nanocellulose with cationic or anionic surface groups, which can remove heavy metal pollutants from aqueous solutions. The above two mechanisms laid a foundation for applying nanocellulose-based MOFs composites in the field of heavy metal ions adsorption.
Abdelhamid & Mathew (2021a) demonstrated a one-step biomineralization approach to prepare the carboxylated ZIFs and then loaded onto filter paper by vacuum filtration. TOCNF is used as a cheap and environmentally friendly template and regulator in composite materials, which can form graded porous structures and provide negatively charged functional groups, and can be used as an effective site for adsorption of positively charged pollution. Owing to the adsorption performance of ZIF-8 and the water absorption/loss capacity characteristics of BC, Ma et al. (2019b) prepared the BC $@$ ZIF-8 aerogel sponge with the removal efficiency of $81\%$ for the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ion pollutants. The rich hydroxyl groups of BC promote the adsorption of metal ions via weak interaction, followed by the nucleation of MOFs on the surface of BC. The BC $@$ ZIF-8 aerogel sponge can be manually compressed to convert to film in order to deplete the absorbed water. When returned the film into the water, the BC@ZIF-8 aerogel sponge recovered its original shape instantly (Fig. 4a and b). Functional modification of MOFs is also a potential method to improve performance. The solvent-assistant linker exchange method was used to functionalize MOF-808 with EDTA to provide more metal chelating sites (Fig. 4c), then cross-linked MOF-808-EDTA with TOCNF was developed to minimize the agglomeration of MOFs (Mo, Shen, Tan, & Zhang, 2021). The MOF-808-EDTA nanoparticles were tight anchoring on the fiber surface of TOCNF due to the hydrogen bonds between carboxylic groups of TOCNF and the carboxyl groups of MOF-808-EDTA. Compared with the un-functionalized samples, the maximum ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ uptake capacity was obviously improved up to $300\mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ . Li et al. (2020b) synthesized a UiO-66-EDTA/CNF/CMC aerogel by introducing EDTA-modified UiO-66 CNF aerogel and doped it with a certain concentration of CMC to create fibrous, cylindrical, and layered pore structures. In particular, it achieved the removal efficiency of more than $91\%$ for nine kinds of heavy metal ions $(\mathbf{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $S\mathrm{n}^{4+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Zr}^{4+},$ using aerogel composite by optimizing the pore structures.
Among these heavy metal pollutants, uranium is unique radioactive element because of its applications in nuclear energy. Once the radioactive element uranium is discharged into seawater or land, it is difficult to degrade and show cumulative effects after being absorbed by humans, animals, and plants (Le Guernic et al., 2016). Zhou et al. (2021) prepared the BC $@$ ZIF-8 carbon aerogels by in-situ growth ZIF-8 on the surface of BC, which was coated with polydopamine and then pyrolyzed. The maximum adsorption capacity of U(VI) can reach $387.13~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ . To further improve the adsorption performance of U(VI), Cheng et al. (2021) ingeniously used the advantage of the BPQDs photocatalytic reduction active for soluble U(VI) to insoluble U(IV). The photogenerated electrons transferred from BPQDs to MOFs and enhanced the separation of photogenerated electron-hole pairs. The lightweight heterojunction aerogel BP@CNF-UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ had an ultra-high adsorption capacity of $858.3~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}$ , which could also remove a variety of heavy metal ions under sunlight simultaneously (Fig. 4d and e). Besides, the CNF provides sufficient H-bonding and physical entanglements interactions with $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , endowing the as-prepared $\mathrm{BC@CNF-\bar{\Omega}^{\prime}\bar{\Omega}O-66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ with $80\%$ compression resistance after eight times compression test.
## 3.2 Dyes and pharmaceutical contamination
Textiles and pharmaceuticals have the characteristics of complex raw materials, diverse production processes, and wide varieties of products. The wastewater from the pharmaceutical and dyeing process is more difficult to be treated due to high pollutant content and poor biodegradability. Pharmaceutical contamination may lead to bio-concentration in the food chain and eventually return to the body through food (Lertxundi, Domingo-Echaburu, & Orive, 2021). Dye pollution is also a thorny problem due to the carcinogenic effect of most dyes, particularly azo dyes, which seriously harm human health (Gordon, 1964). Zhu et al. (2016b) fabricated the
MOF $@$ CNCs hybrid aerogels via sol-gel and the freeze-dried process by combining MOFs and CNCs into porous CMC solution. Thanks to functional MOFs trapped in CNCs and crosslinked to CMC by physical entanglement and Van Der Waals interactions, the crystallization properties of MOFs are well preserved, proving that the structure of MOFs will not collapse after loading.
MOFs can be further pyrolyzed to obtain photocatalytic activity. Ma et al. (2020) developed a hollow CNCs(E)/ZIF-8 hybrid microspheres derived from Pickering emulsion with the 1060.2 $\mathbf{mg\cdotg^{-1}}$ for MG adsorption capacity. In addition, after pyrolyzation at high temperature, carbonized $\mathrm{CNC(E)}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ composite based on porous zinc oxide/carbon showed excellent photocatalytic degradation ability $99.8\%$ for $10~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{J}.^{\cdot}\textbf{M}^{3}$ in the sunlight). Compared with CNCs and CNFs, BC is also used to construct for water pollution treatment due to the high cellulose content. As displayed in Fig. 4f, an in-situ anchoring method was used to prepare the ZIFs@carboxymethylated BC composite films (Mai et al., 2021). In the presence of mono-chloroacetic acid and strong sodium oxide, the surface hydroxyl group of BC is carboxymethylated, giving BC more binding sites with MOFs. The as-prepared composite films had $98.7\%$ removal efficiency for indigo carmine and the tensile strength was up to $24.4\mathrm{MPa}$ .
To deal with pharmaceutical pollution, Abdelhamid and Mathew (2021b) skillfully used TOCNF as an improvement component, effectively improving the specific surface area, pore volume and nitroaromatic compounds catalytic activity of ZIFs/cellulose filter paper composites synthesized in situ by wet chemistry method. Cui et al. (2020) investigated a soft foam-like UiO-66/PDA/BC for the removal of aspirin and TC. These results exhibited the adsorption performance of aspirin $\mathrm{(141.1~mg{\cdot}g^{-1}},$ and TC $(84.3~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}})$ . CWAs are a source of drug pollution that needs to be taken seriously. Seo et al. (2021) aimed to a nerve agent simulant MPO, developing the robust UiO-66/TOCNF aerogel composites. The as-prepared aerogel composite possessed the capability to dispose of $53.7\ \mathrm{g}$ of MPO for one h in the one $\mathrm{m}^{2}$ of the filtering area. In addition to unitary MOFs composites, binary MOFs composites are studied. KarzarJeddi et al. (2020) proposed a chemically modified CNF spherical hydrogel crosslinking with cationic ZIF-67 and anionic HKUST-1 MOFs via H-bonding crosslinking (Fig. 4g), and then dropped in liquid nitrogen and lyophilized to obtain MOFs $@$ CNFs ZABs with the adsorbing capacity of $121.20~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}$ for DIC. The spherical hydrogel had tailorable size and better performance, compared with those of the bulk counterparts.
## 3.3 Oil contaminants
Industrial waste oil is also one of the main factors that harm the environment. Oil pollutants form oil film on the water surface, which limits the gas exchange between the atmosphere and water, hinders the photosynthesis of green plants, and reduces the oxygen source. MOFs can be post-treated by combining hydrophobic bonding, or using hydrophobic substances, which are promising candidates for absorbing oil (Zhu, Zhang, Li, & Zhu, 2017). Li et al. (2020b) fabricated the porous aerogels through freeze-drying CNF suspensions with CMC as crosslinking agent mixed with UiO-66 powder and then coating with MTMS aerogel by chemical vapor deposition. The composite aerogel had the density of $0.009\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , porosity of $97\%$ , and diesel oil adsorption ability for $210\ \mathrm{g{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ with compressibility and brittleness resistance.
The summary of the main features of representative nanocellulose-based MOF composites for sewage disposal is listed in Table 2. Although nanocellulose-based MOFs composites have been widely researched for sewage disposal, some problems still remain to be solved. The number of reuses still needs to be improved such as more than ten times with almost unchanged sewage treatment performance. How to simultaneously filter a variety of pollutants and improve the adsorption/filtration rate is also a performance worthy of attention. The feature of degradability can be fully highlighted, and secondary pollution to the environment can be avoided by selecting appropriate degradable MOFs with large adsorption capacity and nanocellulose. Besides, for the application of oil-water separation, nanocellulose-based MOF $\mathrm{{c^{\prime}.....}{\cdot}{\mathrm{{0s}}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{es}}}$ have great research prospects in the future.
In addition to sewage disposal, environmental pollutant detection is also vital in the environmental field. Early warning can be detected through changes in material luminescence, electrochemical signals, and color. Environmental detection needs to face complicated and unpredictable situations. Zhang et al. (2021c) proposed the environmental detection sensors needs to develop in the direction of high sensitivity, practicability, simplification, and high throughout detection. The development of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites is conducive to the further improvement of environmental pollutant detection materials.
For the drug contamination detection, Wang, Qian, & An (2022) developed a fluorescent responsive nanocellulose composite film by combining TOCNF and Tb coordination polymer. The prepared composite $\sin\alpha$ with the high transparency $(88\%)$ at a low load $(7.2\%)$ . Meanwhile, the sensitive sensing of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions with the detection limit of $0.0392\upmu\mathrm{M}$ is realized. In the aspect of heavy metal ion detection, ZIF-8/TOCNF composite papers were prepared by ex situ and in situ growth method, which can be used as an independent working electrode for highly selective chemical sensing of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions, with detection limits of $40\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $8\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively (Abdelhamid & Mathew, 2021). In addition, since the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the aqueous solution is unknown in actual industrial production, the materials used for environmental detection need to have good pH tolerance. Transparent Eu-MOF $@$ TOCNF fluorescence hybrid film was prepared through in situ synthesis method. The composites possessed brilliant chemical stability under the $\mathrm{\pH}$ range of 3-11, which can act as a self-calibrating luminescent sensor to sense ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in under different pH conditions (Wang, Pei, Qian, & An, 2020). Phenolic compounds are an important industrial raw material, but even wastewater containing very low concentrations of phenols is harmful to human health and the natural growth of aquatic organisms (Mei et al., 2015). Hou et al. (2021) designed the enzyme-mimic colorimetric sensor by one-step solvothermal and in-situ growth method for phenol detection with the detection limit of $0.31\mathfrak{i}_{\sharp\mathbb{N}}$ . Tannic acid was utilized as adhesive agent to link $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C N C}$ and ZIF-8 NPs, CNC was skillfully used as a stable support for the fixation of nanoparticles to avoid the self-agglomeration of core–shell $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ . Besides, Li et al. (2020d) designed a single-enzymatic biofuel cell-based self-powered sensor that is feasible in bisphenol A detection. Unlike traditional biosensors that need extra power, self-powered biosensors use various types of environmental energy (such as biomechanical energy, biochemical energy, and solar energy) to convert into electrical energy, which can operate sustainably without external power. Laccase was successfully encapsulated in ZIF-8, and then combined with bacterial cellulose/carboxylated multi-walled carbon nanotubes skeleton to prepare composite electrode with high flexibility and assembled it into a self-powered biosensor. The designed device can detect bisphenol A effectively, with the detection limit concentration is $1.95{\times}10^{-3}\mathrm{mM}.$
## 4 Nanocellulose-based MOFs composites for gas separation and purification
Gas separation and purification is an essential step in industrial processing and air purification.
The membrane technology or porous foam materials are used for gas separation. The membrane process is more favored in the gas separation field because of the advantages of low cost and easy operation (Park, Kamcev, Robeson, Elimelech, & Freeman, 2017). The porous structure can significantly improve separation efficiency (Galizia et al., 2017). Due to the rich carboxyl group of nanocellulose and the controllable pore size and versatilities in group modification of MOFs, Besides, MOFs have a small size, large specific surface area, and the ordered structure, solving the ―plumbing problem‖ (Thornton et al., 2012). Nanocellulose-based MOFs composites have become a generation of more popular gas separation materials.
## 4.1 Gas separation
With industrial globalization, carbon dioxide emissions are rapidly increasing in the atmosphere, which could cause global warming, sea level rise and extreme weather events (Park et al., 2022). Removing excess impurities to get pure carbon dioxide gas or convert it to carbon materials became the hot spot of scientific research (Ma et al., 2022). The MOFs as functional materials were found to have selective gas adsorption capacity (Datta et al., 2022). Nanocellulose-based MOFs composites are considered for preparing advanced gas filtration membranes for the separation and purification of carbon dioxide.
As early as 2015, Matsumoto et al. (2016) had done pioneering work on the gas-separation applications of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites. The MOF-TOCNF film@paper was synthesized by loaded ZIF-90 onto TOCNF, demonstrating the air tightness of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites to prevent the decrease of gas separation effect. The $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions were introduced into the carboxyl groups of TOCNFs as active sites, and the ZIF-90 crystals were synthesized by strongly interacting with $Z\mathrm{n}$ -carboxylates. The MOF-TOCNF composite film $@$ paper had a significant effect on blocking $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas by over $99\%$ and through $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for the flux of $2.83\times10$ GPU. Jia, Zhang, Feng, Zhou, & Yao (2020) prepared a series of ZIF-8 $@$ CNF membranes by vacuum filtering method based on the chelating effect between the carboxyl group of CNFs and Zn ions of ZIF-8. With the increase of MOF amount from the 0 to $70\mathrm{\mt}\%$ , the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas permeability and selectivity are increased. When the amount of MOF is $70\mathrm{wt}\%$ , ZIF-8 crystals can be uniformly dispersed, forming the connecting channels. When the permeability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is 550 Barrer, it achieves the selectivity of 45.5 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and 36.2 for $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ .
Besides ZIF-8, the UiO-66-NH2/CNF-COOH gas separation composite membrane was prepared for the application of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{\gamma}$ separation (Zhang et al., 2018). The as-obtained UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ /CNF-COOH membrane exhibited the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability of 139 Barrer and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity ratio of 46. The small pore size of MOFs nanoparticles acted as a channel to allow $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to pass through and the intrinsic selectivity of MOFs nanoparticles for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Besides, the crystalline structures of CNFs were acting as the high ${\bf N}_{2}$ -barrier role. In addition to gas separation membranes, $\succeq$ as storage foams were also reported. Because the amino groups were grafted to ZIF-8 particles to enhance the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture ability, the amino-functionalized ZIF-8 was prepared through a mixed linker approach and then in-situ growth on BC substrate. The resulted in ZIF- ${3}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{BC}$ aerogels exhibited $1.63\mathrm{\mmoL{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ adsorption capacity and a ratio of 22 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity. Additionally, the ZIF- ${\cdot8\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{BC}}$ foams can be regenerated by heating at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Shaheed et al., 2021). ZIF-L, as a typical gas storage material, was proved to be capable of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption due to the special sizes of $9.4\mathrm{~\AA~}\times7.0\mathrm{~\AA~}\times5.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ cushion-shaped cavities that are well suited to accommodate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules (Chen et al., 2013). Valencia et al. (2019) fabricated the ultralight and porous ZIF-L/CNFs/gelatin hybrid foams with a density of 37.4 $\mathbf{kg}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}^{-3}$ . The $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions have interacted with hydroxyl and carbonyl groups of TOCNF. ZIF-L/CNFs/gelatin hybrid foams were obtained via binding to gelatin by electrostatic interaction. When the loading rate of ZIF-L was $50\%$ , the adsorption capacity of carbon dioxide reached $0.75~\mathrm{mmoL{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ . In summary, aerogels were used to prepare gas storage materials due to the characteristics of high porosity and large internal surface areas, which could also be used to directly collect the required gas to achieve the purpose of gas separation (Wilson, Gabriel, & Tezel, 2020).
## 4.2 Air purification
Long-term exposure to poor air containing particulate pollutants or VOC will cause various diseases, effecting human health Li, Zhao, Zang, & Li, 2020; Liang et al., 2020). Nanocellulose-based MOFs composites have also reported in the applications of filtering poisonous and harmful gases. The innovative double-component MOF multilayer Ag-MOFs@CNF@ZIF-8 air filters were designed (Ma, Zhang, et al., 2019). The double-component MOFs (ZIF-8 and Ag-MOF) deposited on filters would polarize the surface of fine PM to lead the pollution easily interact with the unbalanced metal ions of MOFs. Besides, the special multilayer intensified the frequency of effective contact between filters and PM. The composite membrane showed a high $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ removal efficiency above $90\%$ . Zhao et al. (2019) used a filtration-assisted gelation process to fabricate a hierarchical porous CNFs stringed
HKUST-1 membrane on a stainless-steel screen. The defects in MOFs and the imbalance of metal ions on the surface can improve the removal efficiency of $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ , and the CNFs connect HKUST-1 in series through the inner and outer parts of the stainless-steel net to form a fine membrane structure, which showed above $95\%$ of $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ particles blocking capacity.
In addition to $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ particulate pollutants, most artificial boards and adhesives will release formaldehyde (A common VOC gas) in indoor furniture and decoration, which is the main source of indoor pollutants that endanger human health (Liang et al., 2020). The CNFs/HKUST-1/stainless steel screen also demonstrated a recyclable formaldehyde adsorption capacity of $47.71~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ while blocking $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ particles for 24 h (Zhao et al., 2019). Zhou et al. (2019) grow continuous MOF layers on CNFs via an interfacial reaction method, which relies on the synergistic effect of the TOCNF and PVP surfactant. The CNF $@$ MOF composite nanopapers were applied for VOC formaldehyde adsorption. After stable operation for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ , the VOC concentration dropped from 100 ppm to 50 ppm, just half the initial concentration. The summary of main features of representative nanocellulose-based MOF composites for gas separation is listed in Table 3. Nanocellulose-based MOF composites have great development for gas separation, and how to adjust the selectivity and flux of gas to the best value is one of the future directions. Under wet conditions, the increase of permeability of nanocellulose membrane will lead to the decrease of separation efficiency, which requires further modification of the nanocellulose (Wu et al., 2022b). The appropriate MOFs should be selected as the separation medium and control the loading rate of the nanocellulose. Besides, the addition of excessive MOFs will lead to the decrease of the mechanical strength of the composites, so choosing the nanocellulose materials with higher strength to cope with the high pressure in the gas separation process is necessary.
## 5 Nanocellulose-based MOF composites for energy
Improving energy storage performance is one of the ways to use energy further effectively.
Supercapacitors and batteries are two popular electrical energy storage devices at present (Du et al., 2022). Recently, green, environmentally, friendly, and portable battery energy storage equipment is one of the development directions (Dühnen et al., 2020; Hu et al., 2022a; Xu et al., 2022). The special structure of MOFs can be preserved after pyrolyzation at an appropriate temperature (Fan et al., 2020), which gives MOFs excellent ion transport capacity (Wu & Guo, 2019). Nanocellulose can be pyrolyzed into carbon materials as a cross line conductive network or as a flexible support framework (Huang et al., 2020; Yuan & Ma, 2021). Hence, nanocellulose-based MOF composites were expected to have applications in the field of electrical energy storage.
## 5.1 Supercapacitors
SCs combine the advantages of the rapid charge-discharge performance of conventional capacitors and the super-high energy storage capacity of batteries (Liu et al., 2022b; Wang et al., 2022c), which can be classified as EDL $\mathbf{C}^{\prime}$ and pseudocapacitors depending on the charge storage mechanism and the electrode material used (Ma, Cao, Xin, Bian, & Ma, 2019; Pourfarzad, Shabani-Nooshabadi, & Ganjali, 2020). Hybrid supercapacitors take full advantage of the electrode materials of EDLCs and pseudocapacitors, and improve the performance of SCs (Simon & Gogotsi, 2020). MOF-based supercapacitors electrode materials both involve EDLCs and pseudocapacitance due to the reversible valance change of metals or metal oxide in MOFs (Du et al., 2021b). Therefore, the nanocellulose-based MOFs composites can be acted as electrode materials for preparing hybrid supercapacitors.
Li et al. (2021) prepared the "frame structure" composite films after pyrolyzation of CNF/GO /ZIF-8 under the nitrogen atmosphere (Fig. 5a). The specific capacitance of carbonized-CNFs/GO/ZIF-8 composite film can reach to $423.8~\mathrm{Fcdotg^{-1}}$ at $5\ \mathrm{mV\cdots^{-1}}$ . The unique carbon structure of GO sheets as the ―rebar‖, CNFs as ―concrete‖, and ZIF-8 as ―bricks‖ improved the electrical conductivity of composite film. Based on the pyrolyzation of BC reinforced by the soft template of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC, Ma et al. (2021) reported the binder-free electrodes after pyrolyzation. In the pyrolyzation process, all micropores, mesoporous, and channels are produced due to the gasification/evaporation etching of zinc. The capacitance of the prepared material is $297~\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ at $1\ \mathrm{A\cdotg^{-1}}$ with the energy and power density of $14.83~\mathrm{Wh{\cdotkg}^{-1}}$ at 0.60 $\mathbf{k}\mathbf{W}{\cdot}\mathbf{k}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Remarkability, the capacitance of the SCs did not change significantly after 65,000 cycles at $6\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , reserving $100\%$ capacitance.
permission (Fu et al., 2022).
Although the pyrolyzation of MOFs and nanocellulose composites are usually carried out in the preparation process to obtain better electrical conductivity, the high-temperature pyrolysis process (1000-1200 K) will lead to relatively high energy consumption (Masyuk, Sherin, Snytnikov, & Snytnikov, 2018) and affect the inherent characteristics of the original MOFs. Therefore, conductive polymer materials are used in the preparation of supercapacitor electrodes to satisfy the development of portable electrodes.
Xu et al. (2018) applied ZIF-67 templated Co-Ni LDH nanocages, CC, and CNTs to prepare the LDH-CC-CNT nanosheets by interweaving method. Due to the flexibility and foldability of CC matrix and electrochemical performance of LHD and CNTs, the areal capacitance of LDH-CC-CNT nanosheets reach $1979~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{2}$ at 1 $\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ . Similarly, the template method was also used. Zhou et al. (2019) used the template-sacrificing strategy to fabricate MOF-PPy/PDA/BC flexible symmetric SC. The presence of ZIF-67 is essential to properly control the coating of PPy to $\hat{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathbf{rm}}$ a conductive channel (Fig. 5b). The flexible symmetric SC shows an areal energy density of $89.8\ \upmu\mathrm{Wh}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a power density of $0.31\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mW}}\cdot\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ . Moreover, Zhou et al. $(\cdot2)19)$ fabricated freestanding CNF $@\mathrm{c}$ -MOF nanopapers with Ni-HHTP and Ni-HITP, two types of conductive MOFs on CNFs via interfacial synthesis method (Fig. 5c). CNFs are completely wrapped by continuous conductive MOF nanolayers. The electrical conductivity of CNF $@\mathrm{c}$ -MOF nanopapers can reach up to $100{\cdot}{\mathrm{S~}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ as well as excellent mechanical properties of tensile stresses up to $350\mathrm{{MPa}}$ . As a kind of energy storage material, the ultimate purpose of supercapacitor is to be applied in practical life to bring convenience to the development of human society. Therefore, it is important to explore more economical devices with lost cost and safety improvements (Zhang et al., 2021d).
## 5.2 Battery
As a common energy storage device in our life, the main components of battery are the positive and negative electrode, diaphragm, and electrolyte (Winter & Brodd, 2004). However, the battery has been developing slowly due to the lack of suitable electrode materials, electrolytes, and the matching battery diaphragm (Armand & Tarascon, 2008). Therefore, using organic batteries made of green and sustainable biomass, and designing more environmentally friendly batteries have become a goal (Larcher & Tarascon, 2015). Nanocellulose-based MOF composites have great prospects as battery materials. Electrode is a key component of battery, and electric energy is generated by the oxidation-reduction reaction between anode and cathode to generate electrons. Different from electrode materials used in supercapacitors, batteries have high requirements. According to different battery types, the electrode material of the battery needs to react and transport with different metal ions (Meng et al., 2017).
Li et al. (2020d) reported a sodium-ion batteries anode material constructed by $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}$ -CNFs derived from ZIFs and BC. After impregnation of BC with cobalt/zinc ions and dimethylimidazole, $\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@$ N-CNFs were synthesized by twice pyrolysis (Fig. 5d). The $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}$ -CNFs presented 864.2 mAh· $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{mA\cdotg^{-1}}$ capacity and extended service cycling life stability on $220.1\ \mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ at $1.6\mathrm{\A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles. The synergistic effect of ZIF-67 $@$ ZIF-8 bimetallic composite and 3D interconnected BC carbon nanofibers produced electrochemical properties. Besides, another component of batteries diaphragm (or named separator, interlayer) separated the positive and negative poles of the battery, preventing them from short-circuiting by contact, which had the function of allowing electrolyte ions to pass through. Ma et al. (2019) prepared BC $@$ UiO-66@PDA aerogels for flexible lithium-sulfur battery interlayer through a rapid and direct in-situ growth method by combining UiO-66, BC, and PDA into a composite aerogel. The galvanostatic charge/discharge curve exhibited BC@UiO-66@PDA interlayer a lower capacity-fading rate with $739\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{C}$ over 100 cycles. By introducing ZIF-67 on the BC nanofibers and vacuum $\dot{\mathbf{u}}_{\mathfrak{c}}\mathrm{'in}_{\mathfrak{s}}$ , Huang and co-worker (Huang, Zhao, & Li, 2021) prepared the lithium-ion battery separator, which showed the 0.837 $\mathrm{{ms}}{\cdot}\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ ionic conductivity and $91.41\%$ at $0.2\mathrm{C}$ discharge capacity retention, heat resistance, and environmental friendliness, in line with the requirements of the battery diaphragm. In addition, the application of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites was also reported in the electrolyte. Compared with liquid electrolytes, solid or gel electrolyte had better advantages (Ma et al., 2022). Fu et al. (2022) reported a porous composite aerogel as the GE matrix, consisting of UiO-66 and BC, to regulate ion deposition and promote ion conduction, to effectively inhibit Li dendrite and stabilize LMBs. The anionphilic linkers of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 can provide added anion-immobilization sites, making the GE more effective in regulating ion deposition. The as-prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 GE exhibited about $1\mathrm{m}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ionic conductivity, $0.82\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ transference number, $4.9\mathrm{V}$ wide electrochemical stability window, and long the cycle life about $1200\mathrm{h}$ (Fig. 5e).
By combining ion-conductive MOFs to fabricate the continued MOF chain, Zeng et al. (2021b) used BC as the bridge to eliminate the interfacial blockage between MOF particles, and constructed the $Z\mathbf{r}$ -BPDC- $2\mathrm{SO_{3}H}$ solid-state electrodes with high-density electronegative groups of $\mathrm{H_{2}B P D C-2S O_{3}H}$ . The result of cross-linked MOF chains showed ionic conductivity of $7.88\times$ ${10}^{-4}{\mathrm{S}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ and a wide electrochemical window up to $5.10~\mathrm{V}.$ Because of the integrated linear ion transport channel and optimized interface compatibility, it had splendid ability to inhibit the growth of lithium dendrite. Hence, nanocellulose-based MOFs composites are expected to play an important role in multifunctional batteries in the future. The summary of the main features of representative nanocellulose-based MOF composites for batteries is listed in Table 4. The battery is similar to a supercapacitor. As a kind of energy storage material, how to reduce the preparation cost and improve safety are essential requirements (Abada et al., 2016; Turcheniuk, Bondarev, Amatucci, & Yushin, 2021). In the subsequent development of energy storage materials, nanocellulose can be developed into flexible and stretchable batteries based on excellent mechanical properties. In addition, with the improvement of environmental awareness and the increased attention to solid-state energy storage devices, nanocellulose-based MOFs composites as degradable gel electrolytes also need to be paid attention to. How to fully optimize the conductive pathway between nanocellulose and MOFs to improve the battery performance is also important.
## Journal Pre-proof
## 6 Nanocellulose-based MOFs composites for biomedical applications
Biomedical materials have been widely used to improve the quality of human life (Deng et al., 2022b; Hu et al., 2022b; Jeswani & Azapagic, 2020). As biomedical materials, the vital aspect is biocompatibility to avoid serious biological allergic reactions or serious toxicity, which will affect the normal growth and development of the organism (Amiri, Khazaeli, Salehabadi, & Salavati-Niasari, 2021; Fu, Qi, Liu, Cao, & Ma, 2018). Due to the small size, nanocellulose-based MOFs composites correspond with the sub-micron substances of the biological world, making them particularly fitting for collaborations at that scale (Hassan et al., 2017; Mendes, Figueira, Leite, Gales, & Almeida Paz, 2020).
## 6.1 Drug delivery
Although the use of nanocellulose as a drug carrier was well developed, there is still deficient in the long-term drug release ability (Ansar et al., 2022). The characteristics of large internal pore volumes and the organic ligand instability of MOFs endow MOFs with drug delivery capabilities and long-term and controlled drug release capabilities (Wen et al., 2021). Therefore, the nanocellulose-based MOFs composites have promising applications in the drug delivery field (Nadar, Vaidya, Maurya, & Rathod, 2019).
Liu et al. (2021c) fabricated the PDA@ZIF-8/CNFs composite hydrogel. The ZIF-8 and TC were in-situ grown on the PDA surface and then physically cross-linked with CNFs (Fig. 6a). The drug delivery time was approximately $85\mathrm{~h~}$ . In addition to the hydrophilic drug, the hydrophobic drug of curcumin was also studied due to the health-promoting ingredient (Liu, Huo, Li, Qin, & Liu, 2021). The composite curcumin hydrogels have the release time of $107\mathrm{~h~}$ , pH responsive-acid sensitive, and near-infrared light sensitive- (accelerated drug release) abilities. Besides, four kinds of models including Higuchi, Korsmeyer-Peppas, zero order, and first order models were used to confirm the anomalous transport drug delivery mechanism of different composite hydrogels.
Furthermore, 3D printing is regarded as a potential technology for producing functional materials to build more accurate structures and achieve large-scale industrial manufacturing (Dai et al., 2022; Firmanda et al., 2022). Nanocellulose has gained popularity in 3D printing due to its suited rheological property (Cao et al., 2019). Sultan et al. (2019) reported the 3D printing for rapidly constructing porous scaffolds by combining TOCNF with ZIF-8 and MIL-100 (Fe) as hybrid inks (Fig. 6b and c). The 3D printing TOCNF scaffolds are easily encapsulated curcumin drug guest molecules in MOFs, achieving up to $30\mathrm{h}$ of drug release capacity. The 3D printing technology provides a way for the preparation of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites for drug delivery in the future.
## 6.2 Antibacterial
Harmful bacteria and microorganisms can cause lasting infection of animal and plant tissues and spoilage of food (Fu et al., 2021). MOFs exhibit antimicrobial behavior through the process of degradation to release metal nodes, and organic ligands play a synergistic effect role in the antibacterial process (Cui et al., 2021; Shen et al., 2020). To avoid causing an allergic reaction that could make the original organism more severe, the same important feature of antibacterial materials is biocompatibility. Nanocellulose has excellent biocompatibility and special surface chemistry as antibacterial material. Taking ECH as a crosslinker, Qian et al. (2018) fabricated the cotton CP/CNF/ZIF-67 composite paper (Fig. 6d). The as-prepared composite paper had the activity against the $E$ . coli with the $12\ \mathrm{mm}$ diameter inhibition zone as well as 17.20 and
1.90 MPa tensile strength under dry and wet conditions, respectively. ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ was widely used as antibacterial material to improve the antibacterial property (Fu, Gao, Qi, Ma, & Li, 2018). Ma et al. (2019c) conducted the air purification materials for antibacterial activity. Because of the leaching behavior of the active $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ metal ions and organic ligands released by the prepared Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@ZIF-8 composite filter, which exhibited antibacterial properties with an inhibition zone diameter of $18.1~\mathrm{mm}$ better than ZIF-8@CNF $@$ CF $(9.8~\mathrm{mm})$ ) and Ag-MOFs $@$ CNF@CF ( $13.2~\mathrm{mm}$ ). ROS are a kind of electron reduction product of oxygen (e.g., $0^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and ·OH) (Tong et al., 2022). Chen et al. (2021) prepared BP@CNF-UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ aerogels with above $90\%$ inhibition efficiency for four types of bacteria (S. aureus, E. coli, B. subtilis, and $P.$ aerugin $,s c$ ) based on the effect of ROS from the MOF/BPQDs heterojunction to transfer ph $,\mathrm{{to}_{\xi}}$ enerated carriers (Fig. 6e).
The field of biomedicine is different from other applications because the composites for such biomedicine act directly on living organisms. It is required to be non-toxic and harmless to living organisms as much as possible. Therefore, using harmless materials like cyclodextrin MOFs is necessary in the future development of biomedicine in nanocellulose-based MOFs composites (Dummert et al., 2022). This can not only give full play to the biocompatibility of nanocellulose and the high drug loading of MOFs composites, but can also reduce the damage to organisms. In addition, the effects of drugs on the body are often potential, so further much the effects of long-term use of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites on the body is vital.
## 7 Nanocellulose-based MOFs composites for other applications
In addition to the environment, energy, and biomedical applications, nanocellulose-based
MOFs composites also show other potential applications.
Slow-release N-fertilizer. As described in section 6, MOFs had a high drug load rate because of the high surface area and the tunable pore structures. Similarly, MOFs displayed great potential use in slow-release N-fertilizers. Liu’s groups used MIL-100 (Fe) and TOCNFs as components to prepare a series of nanocellulose-based MOFs hydrogels for the slow-release N-fertilizer for promoting wheat growth. The MIL-100(Fe) $@$ CNF-SA exhibited the urea loading capacity reaches up to $1.47\ \mathrm{\g\cdotg^{-1}}$ . Meanwhile, the composite hydrogel showed a great water-retention capacity about $17.36\%$ of the soil moisture after 30 days (Guo et al., 2021). Considering the difference of $\mathrm{pH}$ values in soils, it is necessary to design a material that can adjust the fertilizer release ability according to pH value (Wang et al., 2021b). The mechanism and performance of MIL-100(Fe)@CNF-SA responsiveness under different pH conditions were further studied. When the external pH changes, -COOH will exist under acidic conditions but will lose $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ to form -COO- under alkaline conditions, thus resulting in different interaction for unlike urea release rates with the swelling performance of hydrogels. The MIL-100(Fe)@CNF-SA showed obvious pH responsibility with varying volumes of shrinkage and water release amounts.
In addition to pH responsiveness, MIL-100 (Fe) $@$ CNF-SA with temperature responsiveness was prepared using NVCL by free-radical polymerization (Lin et al., 2021). After introducing NVCL, the as-prepared composite hydrogels not only maintained the pH response, but also showed temperature response. Specifically, even in the case of different MIL-100 (Fe) content, all the composite hydrogels exhibited repeatable shrinkage and swelling behaviors between 25 $^\mathrm{{{\circ}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ to $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The temperature-sensitive characteristics can endow hydrogel with controllable water absorption and release capacity at different temperatures in a day, thus more in line with the requirements of realistic agricultural production.
Electromagnetic wave shielding. The working equipment will generate electric current and produce electromagnetic radiation, which not only interferes with other working equipment, but also may cause cells to become cancerous under long-term radiation (Zeng et al., 2020; Zeng, Qiao, Zhang, Liu, & Nyström, 2022). Therefore, shielding electromagnetic wave is of great significance in operating various electronic devices (Chen et al., 2022b). Porous nanocellulose-based MOFs composites derived carbon and magnetism of reduced metal center have abundant interfaces and defects, which improve the conductivity of composites, resulting in electromagnetic wave multiple reflections, scattering, and adsorption (Deng, Tang, Wu, Zhang, & Yu, 2021; Shu, Cao, & Cao, 2021). Furthermore, electromagnetic shielding materials dominated by reflection are more attractive because most electromagnetic waves are absorbed rather than reflected, thus avoiding secondary pollution to the environment.
Fei’s group reported two lightweight aerogels containing nanocellulose (CNFs and BC) and ZIF-67 derived carbon (Fei, Liang, Yan, Chen, & Zou, 2020; Fei, Liang, Zhou, Chen, & Zou, 2020). The pyrolyzed 3D carbon nanofiber skeleton contains abundant free electrons, which leads to large electron migration and further leads to conduction loss. Moreover, magnetic loss and interfacial polarization occur at the interface among nanocellulose, porous structure, and cobalt crystal derived from ZIF-67 and polyhedral skeletons. Two composites showed electromagnetic shielding performance of 35.1 dB and 56.07 dB in whole X-band, respectively.
In addition to aerogel, the film also played an important role in the electromagnetic shielding field. Yuan et al. (2022) optimized the structural design and prepared an asymmetric electromagnetic absorbing composite membrane by vacuum filtration and hot pressing. CNFs were used as the carrier, the upper layer was carbonized ZIF-67 particles, and the lower layer was graphene. The as-prepared $0.1\ \mathrm{mm}$ C-ZIF67/GNP composite film exhibited the shielding effectiveness up to 50.5 dB based on asymmetric structure for absorption-reflection-reabsorption shielding mechanism as well as tensile strength of 46.33 MPa.
Luminescent materials. Luminescent materials have a wide range of applications like systems for emitting displays, fluorescent lamps, and light-emitting diodes, and a system for detecting X-rays or gamma rays (Ronda, 2017). Ln-MOFs have the characteristics of the high quantum efficiency, long lifetime and linear spectrum. However, the harsh luminescence modulation, low transparency, and poor film formation limited the development of Ln-MOFs (Schäferling & Resch-Genger, 2017). The above problem can be solved by hybridizing Ln organic phosphors into polymer substrates by covalent, coordination, or physical doping (Zhang, Liu, Chang, Li, & Zhang, 2019). Zhang et al. (2021e) researched luminescence modulation and prepared the transparent and hazy nanopapers via Ln-MOF modified TOCNFs. The color-tuning, photo-switching, and white light-emitting luminescent nanopapers can take on different colors by controlling the control of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}/{\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ molar ratio and excitation wavelength. However, recent research showed that the 3D Ln-MOFs with polymer matrix hybridization could induce stress concentration, which changed from tough to brittle and decreased the mechanical performance of as-prepared materials. Therefore, the 3D supramolecular single crystals were further processed via the ultrasonic peeling to prepare 2D Ln-MOFs. Chang et al. (2020) prepared 2D Ln nanopapers with a high haze from $26\%$ to $93\%{\sim}95\%$ , compared with pure nanopaper. Besides, 2D Ln nanopapers have the characteristics of $>90\%$ transparency and $>28\ \mathrm{MPa}$ mechanical properties.
Besides, the CNCs induce structural colors through self-assembly due to the light interference, scattering, or diffraction of physical color. Zheng et al. (2021) controlled the chemical surface of CNC and assembled with ZIF-8 to decrease the particle number to a single-digit level to enhance the EQY.  CNDs were further introduced to avoid light intensity reduction caused by ZIF-8 extinction. The EQY of as-prepared CND@ZIF-8@CNC reaches $64.84\%$ as well as possible chemical modification and biocompatibility.
## 8 Conclusion and outlook
In summary, there is significant progress in the applications of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites in the fields such as sewage disposal, gas separation, energy storage, biomedical and so on. Some representative examples were used to introduce the synthetic methods, properties, and development of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites in these fields.
Although nanocellulose-based MOFs composites exhibit the advantages in these application fields, some challenges remain to be solved (Fig. 7). Based on our knowledge, future research trends and directions of the nanocellulose-based MOFs composites for more wide applications are expected as follows: (1) Improving the shortcomings of MOFs, such as stability. For example, in the humid environment, water molecules are easy to attack relatively weak metal organic coordination bonds, resulting in phase transition or structural collapse of MOF crystals and reducing the adsorption capacity and selectivity of MOFs (Feng et al., 2015). (2) In-depth of the understanding the different characteristics of nanocellulose among CNFs, CNCs, and BCs to develop nanocellulose-based MOFs. Theoretically, calculation and structural design should be carried out to understand the requirements of a specific responses to characteristics of nanocellulose-based MOFs. Designing the loading ratio of MOFs in nanocellulose to obtain the desired performance. (3) Using more kinds of organic frameworks materials to construct nanocellulose-based organic frameworks composites, such as HOFs, COFs, MCOFs, and CCOFs. Although thousands of kinds of MOFs have been designed, some of them are not really applied in the composites. The universally applicable structural/functional design theories should be proposed and universally applicable research methods should be developed. (4) Exploring the more detailed interaction mechanism between nanocellulose and MOFs. Most of the reported ways of binding MOF materials to nanocellulose are hydrogen bonding, electrostatic interaction and coordination binding (Zhu et al., 2018). Lately, Peterson et al. (2021) divided the methods of
MOF into five types of embedded, diverged, attached, bulged, and agglomerated, which provide an important insight into the process of combination. The effect of hydrogen bond competition between nanocellulose, reaction solution and ligand on the nucleation and growth of MOFs still needs to be investigated. (5) Improving the properties of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites. The key factor is to achieve nanocellulose-based MOFs composites with uniform dispersion and more open active sites, further increasing the properties of composites. For example, MCOFs have open metal sites and stable structures, which can be used as special recognition centers for detection and catalysis. It is still a great challenge to link the physical and chemical properties of composites with their atomic structures. (6) Developing more applications of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites. There are reports on the applications of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites including energy storage, catalysis, gas storage and separation, drug delivery, sensing, and antibacterial (Su et al., 2020; Ye, Jiang, Li, Wu, & Chen, 2021; Zhao et al., 2021). More and more application fields need to be explored. (7) Achieving large-scale production nanocellulose-based MOFs composites. At present, nanocellulose-based MOFs composites have promising applications in various fields, the specific industrial application is lacking. Moreover, reducing the preparation cost to meet the needs of industrialization is also an urgent challenge. Designing greener processes of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites is important using degradable raw materials in environmentally friendly solutions. The realization of these targets will greatly expand the applications of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites whether in laboratory or on the industrial scale. With the development of technology, the synthetic methods and performances of multifunctional nanocellulose-based MOFs composites will be optimized to meet the requirements of applications. The nanocellulose-based MOFs composites which can be completely biodegradable are developed, and the characteristics of nanocellulose such as high mechanical strength, abundant natural sources and biological friendliness are fully exerted. As well as the characteristics of MOFs, such as large specific surface area, wide varieties, and strong functional expansibility, MOFs are expected to play an important role in the fields of environment, energy, biomedicine, military industry, and food in the future. We look forward to the rapid progress in this exciting frontier of nanocellulose-based MOFs composites in the future.
